abstract: >
  DeepCatch v2.3.0 is an open-source cfDNA fragmentomics benchmark that is
  validated on public data the model has never been trained on. A 5-channel
  DELFI profile (5 Mb / 100 kb ratios and coverage, plus a 196-bin fragment-size
  distribution) is extracted from publicly served FinaleDB fragment files and
  scored with a per-study harmonised PCA + logistic-regression pipeline.
  Training on FinaleDB publications 6 + 8 (n=627) and testing on held-out
  publications 1, 7 and 9 gives an external AUC of 0.9444 [0.8731, 1.0000] on
  one whole-genome publication (n=31), 0.708 [0.5148, 0.9012] on a second
  (n=52), and 0.7385 [0.6281, 0.8488] pooled over the publications carrying
  both classes; the whole-exome publication transfers only partly (breast
  0.6938, prostate 0.7127). Transfer between whole-genome studies is real but
  far from uniform, and the assay platform blunts it further. A leave-one-study-out run inside the
  training cohort gives 0.6805, so the pooled 0.9684 is reported as a
  within-cohort separation number rather than a generalisation claim. The
  multi-modal stage is carried by a regularized late fusion (AUC 0.9825) and a
  Bayesian model average (0.9650) rather than the neural encoder (0.554) at
  this sample size. The methylation path runs end to end on real public
  fragment data. Not clinically validated; research use only.
